\\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\) (the left segment).  \n   - If \\(i<n\\), place a tile covering the cells \\((i,i+1),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) (the right segment).  \n\n   The row \\(i=1\\) has no left tile, and the row \\(i=n\\) has no right tile.  \n   Hence the total number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The tiles are pairwise disjoint, lie on grid lines, and together cover every unit square except the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\); each row and each column therefore has exactly one uncovered square.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower\u2011bound argument shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nIn each row and each column there must be exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a **permutation matrix**: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\n\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery unit square of the board belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); the uncovered squares are precisely the complement of \\(L\\cup R\\).  \nA rectangular tile cannot contain an uncovered square, therefore each tile is completely contained in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\).  \nConsequently the total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }L\\;+\\;\\#\\text{tiles covering }R .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left part \\(L\\)\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\ni_k\\;:=\\;\\text{the unique index with }p(i_k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, such an index exists and is unique.  \nThe cell \\((i_k,k)\\) satisfies \\(k<p(i_k)=k+1\\); hence \\((i_k,k)\\in L\\).\n\nConsider any axis\u2011parallel rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq L\\).  \nSuppose that \\(Q\\) contains two distinct cells of the set  \n\n\\[\nS_L:=\\{(i_k,k)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet these cells be \\((i_k,k)\\) and \\((i_{k'},k')\\) with \\(k<k'\\).  \nSince \\(Q\\) is a rectangle, its column interval contains all integers from \\(k\\) to \\(k'\\); in particular it contains the column \\(k+1\\).  \nIts row interval contains the row \\(i_k\\); therefore the cell \\((i_k,k+1)\\) lies inside \\(Q\\).  \nBut \\((i_k,k+1)=(i_k,p(i_k))\\) is exactly the uncovered square in row \\(i_k\\), which is not allowed to be covered.  \nThus \\(Q\\) cannot contain two distinct points of \\(S_L\\).\n\nHence **each rectangle covering a part of \\(L\\) contains at most one element of \\(S_L\\)**.  \nSince \\(|S_L|=n-1\\), any covering of \\(L\\) by rectangles needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }L\\;\\ge\\;n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right part \\(R\\)\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nj_k\\;:=\\;\\text{the unique index with }p(j_k)=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(p(j_k)=n-k<n-k+1\\), so the cell \\((j_k,n-k+1)\\) lies to the right of the uncovered square in row \\(j_k\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n(j_k,n-k+1)\\in R .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_R:=\\{(j_k,n-k+1)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nExactly the same argument as above shows that a rectangle contained in \\(R\\) cannot contain two distinct points of \\(S_R\\): if it contained \\((j_k,n-k+1)\\) and \\((j_{k'},n-k'+1)\\) with \\(k<k'\\), then the rectangle would also contain \\((j_k,n-k)\\), which is the uncovered square \\((j_k,p(j_k))\\).  \n\nTherefore each rectangle covering a part of \\(R\\) contains at most one element of \\(S_R\\), and because \\(|S_R|=n-1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }R\\;\\ge\\;n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nAdding (1) and (2) yields\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles in any tiling}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling that meets the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) we proceed as follows.\n\n*If \\(i>1\\):* place a tile covering the cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the whole left segment of the row.\n\n*If \\(i<n\\):* place a tile covering the cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the whole right segment of the row.\n\nThe row \\(i=1\\) has no left segment, and the row \\(i=n\\) has no right segment; consequently the total number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nAll tiles are axis\u2011parallel rectangles whose interiors consist solely of covered squares; they are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every unit square except the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \nThus each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality\n\nInequality (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
