ectangles are necessary.  \n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - For every row \\(i>1\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\) (the left part of the row).  \n   - For every row \\(i<n\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells \\((i,i+1),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) (the right part of the row).  \n\n   The row \\(i=1\\) needs no left tile, and the row \\(i=n\\) needs no right tile.  \n   Hence the total number of tiles is  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]  \n   These tiles are pairwise disjoint, lie on grid lines, and together cover every unit square except the diagonal cells; thus each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number of tiles, so the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nIn each row and each column there must be exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nHence the set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered by tiles.  \nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; therefore a tile is a product  \n\n\\[\nI\\times J,\\qquad I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of consecutive integers.  \nThe condition “no uncovered square is covered’’ is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n(I\\times J)\\cap U=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A set of forced squares\n\nFor e