 exchanging the roles of rows and columns; the same argument yields a contradiction.\n\n---\n\n**Case 3.** One square is \\(d_i\\) and the other is \\(e_j\\).  \nWithout loss of generality assume \\(d_i=(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) (the alternative subcase \\((i+1,\\sigma(i))\\) is analogous).  \nHence \\(R\\) contains row \\(i\\) and column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\).\n\nSince \\(e_j\\in R\\), the rectangle also contains a row \\(r\\in\\{\\sigma^{-1}(j),\\sigma^{-1}(j+1)\\}\\) and a column \\(c\\in\\{j,j+1\\}\\).  \nConsequently the row interval \\(I\\) contains both \\(i\\) and \\(r\\), and the column interval \\(J\\) contains both \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(c\\).  \nBecause intervals of integers are convex, \\(I\\) contains the whole segment between \\(i\\) and \\(r\\); in particular it contains the missing row \\(i+1\\).  \nSimilarly, \\(J\\) contains the whole segment between \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) and \\(c\\); therefore it contains the missing column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nThus \\(R\\) contains both rows \\(i,i+1\\) and both columns \\(\\sigma(i),\\sigma(i+1)\\), and consequently the hole \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) lies inside \\(R\\), a contradiction.\n\nAll three cases lead to a contradiction; therefore a hole\u2011free rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEvery tile is a hole\u2011free rectangle, so by the Lemma each tile can cover at most one square of \\(S\\).  \nSince \\(|S|=2n-2\\), any tiling of the board must use at least  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define two rectangles  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\nL_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n- \\(U_k\\) is a \\(k\\times (n-k)\\) rectangle situated entirely above the diagonal.  \n- \\(L_k\\) is an \\((n-k)\\times k\\) rectangle situated entirely below the diagonal.\n\nThe families \\(\\{U_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery off\u2011diagonal square \\((i,j)\\) belongs to exactly one of them:\n\\[\n(i,j)\\in\n\\begin{cases}\nU_i, &\\text{if }i<j,\\\\\nL_j, &\\text{if }i>j.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nHence the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles in (7) tile precisely the set of covered squares and avoid all diagonal holes.\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimal number of tiles  \n\nThe lower bound (6) and the explicit construction both give the same number.  \nTherefore the minimal possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor the original board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
