ve a common index equal to \\(1\\); consequently the diagonal entries of \\(u v^{\\mathsf T}\\) are all \\(0\\).\n\nThus every tile is represented by a rank‑1 binary matrix with zero diagonal.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Matrix of covered cells  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM=\\bigl(m_{ij}\\bigr)_{1\\le i,j\\le n},\\qquad \nm_{ij}=\n\\begin{cases}\n0 & i=j,\\\\[2pt]\n1 & i\\neq j.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n\\(M\\) records precisely the cells that must be covered (all off‑diagonal cells).  \n\nIf the covered cells are partitioned into \\(t\\) tiles, then there exist binary vectors \\(u_k,v_k\\;(k=1,\\dots ,t)\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nM=\\sum_{k=1}^{t} u_k v_k^{\\mathsf T},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand the summands have pairwise disjoint supports (each off‑diagonal entry of \\(M\\) equals \\(1\\) in exactly one summand).  \nEach summand \\(u_k v_k^{\\mathsf T}\\) has rank at most \\(1\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le \\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(u_k v_k^{\\mathsf T})\\le t.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence any tile partition must satisfy \\(t\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\)\n\nWrite \\(J\\) for the all‑ones \\(n\\times n\\) matrix and \\(I\\) for the identity matrix.  \nThen \\(M=J-I\\).\n\nThe eigenvalues of \\(J\\) are \\(n\\) (once) and \\(0\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  \nConsequently the eigenvalues of \\(M=J-I\\) are\n\n\\[\nn-1\\quad\\text{(once)},\\qquad -1\\quad\\text{(with multiplicity }n-1\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nBoth eigenvalues are non‑zero, so \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n\\).\n\nFrom (2) we obtain the lower bound  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge n.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus at least \\(n\\) tiles are necessary.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  A construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. the cells \\((R_i,C_i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single horizontal tile covering all cells of that row except the diagonal one:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Tile }T_i = \\{(R_i,C_j)\\mid j\