\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n     \\]\n   The \\(V_j\\)’s cover all squares strictly below the diagonal, the \\(H_i\\)’s cover all squares strictly above the diagonal, and the two families are disjoint. There are \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles, and they avoid all diagonal holes. Hence \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**  \n   The lower bound and the construction coincide, so the minimal possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of the uncovered unit squares can be described by a permutation\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\\text{the uncovered square in row }i\\text{ is at column }\\sigma(i).\n\\]\nThus each row \\(i\\) contains the two (possibly empty) covered intervals  \n\n\\[\nL_i=[1,\\sigma(i)-1],\\qquad R_i=[\\sigma(i)+1,n],\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nand each column \\(j\\) contains the two (possibly empty) covered intervals  \n\n\\[\nU_j=[1,\\sigma^{-1}(j)-1],\\qquad D_j=[\\sigma^{-1}(j)+1,n],\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\) is the unique row whose hole lies in column \\(j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (four boundary rectangles)\n\nIn any tiling satisfying the condition there exist four pairwise distinct\nrectangles, each having a side on a different side of the board (top,\nbottom, left, right).\n\n*Proof.*  \nColumn 1 contains exactly one hole; let it be at row \\(r\\) (\\(\\sigma(r)=1\\)).\nThe squares of column 1 above the hole are the unit squares \\((i,1)\\) with\n\\(1\\le i<r\\).  Since a rectangle cannot contain the hole \\((r,1)\\), these\nsquares must be covered by a rectangle whose **right** side lies on column 1.\nAnalogously the squares below the hole (\\(r<i\\le n\\)) are covered by a\nrectangle whose **left** side lies on column 1.  At least one of those two\nrectangles has its left side on the left boundary of the board.\n\nExactly the same argument applied to column \\(n\\) (hole at row\n\\(s\\) with \\(\\sigma(s)=n\\)) yields a rectangle having its right side on the\nright boundary.  Applying the argument to row 1 (hole at column\n\\(\\sigma(1)\\)) gives a rectangle with its top side on the top boundary, and\nto row \\(n\\) (hole at column \\(\\sigma(n)\\)) gives a rectangle with its\nbottom side on the bottom boundary.\n\nA rectangle cannot have both a top side and a bottom side on the boundary,\notherwise it would span all rows and would contain a hole; similarly it\ncannot have both a left side and a right side on the boundary.  Hence the four\nrectangles obtained above are distinct. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive lower bound\n\nWe prove that any tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board with one uncovered square\nin each row and column uses at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n*Base cases.*  \nFor \\(n=1\\) there are no covered squares, so \\(0=2\\cdot1-2\\) tiles are needed.  \nFor \\(n=2\\) the board consists of the two covered squares \\((1,2)\\) and\n\\((2,1)\\); they cannot belong to a common rectangle, so at least two\nrectangles are necessary, and \\(2=2\\cdot2-2\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for size \\(n-2\\) (\\(n\\ge3\\)).  Consider an\n\\(n\\times n\\) board with a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of holes.  By Lemma 1 there\nexist four distinct rectangles \\(R_{\\text{top}},R_{\\text{bottom}},\nR_{\\text{left}},R_{\\text{right}}\\) each having a side on a different side of\nthe board.\n\nThe rectangle \\(R_{\\text{top}}\\) covers all squares of the top row\n(row 1) except the hole at \\((1,\\sigma(1))\\); similarly\n\\(R_{\\text{bottom}}\\) covers all squares of row \\(n\\) except its hole,\n\\(R_{\\text{left}}\\) covers all squares of column 1 except its hole, and\n\\(R_{\\text{right}}\\) covers all squares of column \\(n\\) except its hole.\nConsequently the four rectangles together cover **every** square of the outer\nborder (the first and last rows and columns) except the four holes that lie\non those rows and columns.\n\nDelete the first and last rows and the first and last columns.  The remaining\nboard is an \\((n-2)\\times (n-2)\\) rectangle.  The uncovered squares that were\noriginally in the interior rows \\(2,\\dots ,n-1\\) and interior columns\n\\(2,\\dots ,n-1\\) are still uncovered, and each interior row and column still\ncontains exactly one of them.  The rectangles of the original tiling that are\nnot among the four boundary rectangles intersect this interior board and\nform a tiling of it satisfying the same condition.  By the induction\nhypothesis this interior tiling uses at least \\(2(n-2)-2\\) rectangles.\n\nAdding the four boundary rectangles we obtain\n\\[\nt_{\\min}\\ge\\bigl(2(n-2)-2\\bigr)+4=2n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board requires at least\n\\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal:\n\\[\n\\text{hole }(i,i)\\quad\\text{for }i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Below the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nIts height is \\(n-j\\) and its width is \\(1\\).  In any row \\(i>j\\) the hole is at\ncolumn \\(i\\ge j+1\\); therefore column \\(j\\) lies strictly to the left of the\nhole and \\(V_j\\) contains no uncovered square.  The rectangles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\)\nare pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares with column index\n\\(j<i\\), i.e. all squares strictly **below** the diagonal.\n\n*Above the diagonal.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the horizontal rectangle\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nIts height is \\(1\\) and its width is \\(n-i\\).  In any column \\(j>i\\) the hole\nlies at row \\(j\\ge i+1\\); thus column \\(j\\) is strictly below the hole and\n\\(H_i\\) contains no uncovered square.  The rectangles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\)\nare pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares with row index\n\\(i<j\\), i.e. all squares strictly **above** the diagonal.\n\nThe families \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) are disjoint from each other,\nbecause a square either satisfies \\(j<i\\) (belongs to some \\(V_j\\)) or\n\\(i<j\\) (belongs to some \\(H_i\\)), never both.  Hence the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\\[\nV_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},\\; H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nform a tiling of the whole board that leaves exactly the diagonal squares\n(4) uncovered.  Consequently \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nFrom (3) we have the universal lower bound \\(t_{\\min}\\ge2n-2\\); the construction\nabove shows that \\(t_{\\min}\\le2n-2\\).  Hence the minimal possible number of\ntiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\nt_{\\min}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
