## Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet the board be the set  \n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\qquad n=2025 .\n\\]\n\nMatilda must choose a set \\(U\\subset B\\) of uncovered unit squares such that each row and each column contains exactly one element of \\(U\\).  \nHence there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\sigma(i)-1,\\qquad R_i=n-\\sigma(i).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe squares of row \\(i\\) that lie left of the uncovered square are precisely  \n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le L_i\\},\n\\]\nand those that lie right of it are  \n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}\n   =\\{(i,j)\\mid L_i+2\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, the multiset \\(\\{L_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); the same holds for \\(\\{R_i\\}\\).  \nConsequently exactly one row has \\(L_i=0\\) (the row whose uncovered square lies in column 1) and exactly one row has \\(R_i=0\\) (the row whose uncovered square lies in column \\(n\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Every tile lies entirely on one side of the holes\n\nConsider any rectangular tile \\(T\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) contains squares from two different rows \\(i\\) and \\(i'\\), then the column interval of \\(T\\) must be a set \\([c_1,c_2]\\) with \\(c_1\\le c_2\\).  \nSince each of those rows contains a single uncovered square, the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) is forbidden in row \\(i\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')\\) is forbidden in row \\(i'\\).  \nIf \\([c_1,c_2]\\) intersected both the left‑hand part and the right‑hand part of a row, the tile would contain the uncovered square of that row, which is prohibited.  \nHence for all rows intersected by \\(T\\) the whole column interval must lie either strictly left of the uncovered square of each of those rows or strictly right of it.  \nThus each tile is wholly contained in the left‑hand region or wholly in the right‑hand region.\n\nConsequently the tiling of the whole board splits into a tiling of the left‑hand region\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le L_i\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nand a tiling of the right‑hand region\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;L_i+2\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the left‑hand region\n\nFor each row with \\(L_i>0\\) denote its *rightmost* cell by \\((i,L_i)\\).  \nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells, because precisely one row has \\(L_i=0\\).\n\n**Claim.** A single rectangle can contain at most one rightmost cell.  \n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(Q\\) contains rightmost cells \\((i,L_i)\\) and \\((i',L_{i'})\\) with \\(L_i<L_{i'}\\).  \nSince \\(Q\\) is axis‑parallel, its column interval must contain the whole interval \\([L_i,\\,L_{i'}]\\).  \nConsequently \\(Q\\) would also contain the cell \\((i, L_{i'})\\).  \nBut row \\(i\\) has no square in column \\(L_{i'}\\) (its left‑hand part stops at column \\(L_i\\)), so \\((i, L_{i'})\\) is not an allowed square – a contradiction. ∎\n\nBecause each rightmost cell must be covered and each rectangle can cover at most one of them, any tiling of \\(L\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n\nBy the horizontal symmetry (reflect the board in a vertical line) the same argument shows that any tiling of the right‑hand region \\(R\\) also needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nHence any tiling of the whole board requires at least  \n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the lower bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL_i=i-1,\\qquad R_i=n-i .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n**Left‑hand tiling.