least n-1 rectangles.\n\nApply same to R region (by symmetry). So total at least 2(n-1) = 4048 tiles.\n\nConstruction: Choose σ(i)=i (main diagonal uncovered). Then L_i = i-1, R_i = n-i. For left side: for each column c from 1 to n-1, place vertical rectangle T_c^L = {(i,c) | i = c+1,...,n}. This covers all squares left of diagonal. For right side: for each column c from 2 to n, place vertical rectangle T_c^R = {(i,c) | i = 1,...,c-1}. This covers all squares right of diagonal. These are disjoint and total number = (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)=4048. So bound attained.\n\nThus answer 4048.\n\nWe need to verify answer correctness, reasoning validity, and presentation.\n\nFirst, answer correctness: The solution claims minimum number of tiles is 4048. Is that correct? Let's think: The problem asks for minimum number of tiles such that each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square. The solution uses a construction with 4048 tiles, and proves lower bound of 4048. So answer seems correct.\n\nWe need to verify if any hidden flaw: The lower bound argument uses the fact that {L_i} = {0,...,n-1}. Is that always true? Let's check: Since σ is a permutation of {1,...,n}, the values σ(i) are a permutation of 1..n. Then L_i = σ(i)-1 yields values 0..n-1 as a permutation. So yes. Similarly R_i = n - σ(i) yields values 0..n-1 as a permutation. So correct.\n\nLemma 1: They claim that any rectangle cannot contain squares on both sides of the uncovered column for any row it covers. The proof: If rectangle T covers rows a..b and columns c..d. For each row i in [a,b], either d < σ(i) or c > σ(i). If for some i we have d < σ(i) and for some i' we have c > σ(i'), then we have c ≤ d (since rectangle's columns satisfy c ≤ d). Then we have σ(i') < c ≤ d < σ(i). So σ(i') < σ(i). Since σ is a permutation, it's possible that σ(i') < σ(i). But does that lead to contradiction? Let's examine: They claim that this would contradict σ(i') and σ(i) being integers? Actually the condition c ≤ d is 