les for those squares, total at least 4. So lower bound 4 holds for n=3. So maybe the lower bound is correct for n=3. But we need to check if the lemma holds for general n.\n\nThe lemma's claim that any rectangle cannot intersect both L and R is false as we saw for n=3 with rows 1-2 and column 2 rectangle. However, that rectangle intersected L for row2? Let's examine: For σ = identity (diagonal). Row1 uncovered at col1, left region L1 empty, right region R1 columns 2-3. Row2 uncovered at col2, left region L2 = column1, right region R2 columns 3. Row1's L region is empty, R region includes column2. Row2's L region includes column1, R region includes column3. The rectangle covering rows 1-2, column2: For row1, column2 is in R region (since right of uncovered). For row2, column2 is uncovered (since uncovered at column2). Actually column2 is uncovered for row2, so rectangle would cover uncovered square, not allowed. So that rectangle is not allowed. So my earlier example of rectangle covering rows 1-2, column2 is invalid because it includes uncovered square for row2. So the rectangle that straddles both sides must avoid uncovered squares for all rows it covers. For that to happen, the column interval must avoid all σ(i) for i in the row set. So the rectangle's column interval must be a subset of columns that are not among the σ(i) for those rows. Since σ(i) are distinct, there are n - (b-a+1) columns available. So if we pick a set of rows, we can choose columns that avoid those uncovered columns. For the rectangle to intersect both L and R, we need that for some rows the rectangle's column interval is left of σ(i) (i.e., column < σ(i)), and for other rows it's right of σ(i) (i.e., column > σ(i)). But if the column interval is a single interval, and we avoid all σ(i), then either all σ(i) are to the right of the interval (so interval is left of all uncovered columns for those rows) or all σ(i) are to the left of the interval (so interval is right of all uncovered columns