                "content": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n- **Lower bound:**  Observe that in any column the unique uncovered square (the “hole”) splits the column into at most two contiguous blocks of covered cells. Each non‑empty block must be covered by a distinct tile because a tile cannot contain a hole. Exactly two columns have their hole in the top or bottom row (the holes in the top row and bottom row are in distinct columns), so those two columns need only one tile each, while the remaining \\(n-2\\) columns need two tiles each. Hence any tiling uses at least \\(2(n-2)+2 = 2n-2\\) tiles.  \n- **Upper bound:**  Choose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\). For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) (the part **below** the hole). For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) (the part **above** the hole). These two families consist of \\(n-1\\) tiles each, are pairwise disjoint, and together cover every unit square except the diagonal holes. Thus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.  \n- Since the lower bound equals the construction, the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this gives \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe have an \\(n\\times n\\) board of unit squares.  \nA **tile** is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; a tile may consist of any positive integer number of unit squares.  \nExactly one unit square in each row and each column must remain uncovered; denote the set of uncovered squares by  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThus \\(H\\) is a permutation matrix: each row and each column contains precisely one hole.\n\nA tiling of the board is a partition of the set  \n\n\\[\nB:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\}\\setminus H\n\\]\n\ninto axis‑parallel rectangles (tiles).  \nWe must determine the smallest possible number of tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A universal lower bound\n\nFix a column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nLet the unique hole in this column be \\((r_c,c)\\) where \\(r_c=\\pi^{-1}(c)\\).  \nThe column \\(c\\) is then divided into at most two contiguous blocks of covered cells:\n\n* the **upper block** \\(\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i< r_c\\}\\) (empty if \\(r_c=1\\));\n* the **lower block** \\(\\{(i,c)\\mid r_c< i\\le n\\}\\) (empty if \\(r_c=n\\)).\n\nA tile is a rectangle; consequently a single tile cannot contain the hole \\((r_c,c)\\).  \nHence each non‑empty block must be covered by a distinct tile.\n\nExactly one hole lies in the top row (row \\(1\\)) and exactly one hole lies in the bottom row (row \\(n\\)).  \nConsequently there are exactly two columns whose holes are at an endpoint of the column; for those columns one of the two blocks is empty, so they require **one** tile each.  \nAll remaining \\(n-2\\) columns have their hole in an interior row, so both the upper and lower blocks are non‑empty and each of those columns requires **two** tiles.\n\nSumming over all columns we obtain the inequality\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2\\,(n-2)+2 \\;=\\;2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the **identity permutation** \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{down}}_j \\;:=\\;\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R^{\\text{down}}_j\\) is a vertical strip of height \\(n-j\\) lying **below** the hole \\((j,j)\\); it contains no hole because the only hole in column \\(j\\) is at row \\(j\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{up}}_j \\;:=\\;\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R^{\\text{up}}_j\\) is a vertical strip of height \\(j-1\\) lying **above** the hole \\((j,j)\\); again it contains no hole.\n\nThe families \\(\\{R^{\\text{down}}_j\\}_{j=1}^{\\,n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R^{\\text{up}}_j\\}_{j=2}^{\\,n}\\) consist of \\(n-1\\) rectangles each, are pairwise disjoint, and together cover every cell of the board except the diagonal holes.  Indeed, any cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs either to the set \\(\\{i>j\\}\\) (hence to \\(R^{\\text{down}}_j\\)) or to the set \\(\\{i<j\\}\\) (hence to \\(R^{\\text{up}}_j\\)).\n\nThus we have exhibited a tiling with exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Hence the lower bound is tight and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Numerical answer for the problem\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 \\;=\\;2\\cdot2025-2 \\;=\\;4050-2 \\;=\\;4048.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the smallest number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place while satisfying the condition is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
