            "content": ".\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares:** Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix; i.e. there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\}\\).  \n2. **Lower bound \\(2n-2\\):**  \n   - For every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) lies to the left of the uncovered square in that row. Any tile covering this square cannot extend to column \\(\\sigma(i)\\); consequently its rightmost column is exactly \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\). Hence each column \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) (\\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\)) is the rightmost column of a distinct “left‑type’’ tile. There are \\(n-1\\) such columns, so at least \\(n-1\\) left‑type tiles are needed.  \n   - Symmetrically, for every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) lies to the right of the uncovered square. Any tile covering it must have leftmost column \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\). Thus each column \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) (\\(\\sigma(i)<n\\)) is the leftmost column of a distinct “right‑type’’ tile, giving at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles.  \n   - A tile cannot be simultaneously left‑type and right‑type (its columns cannot be both entirely left of and entirely right of the missing squares of its rows). Therefore the total number of tiles is at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).  \n3. **Construction attaining the bound:** Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).  \n   - Below the diagonal (\\(i>j\\)) tile each column \\(j\\) with the vertical strip \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); this yields \\(n-1\\) tiles.  \n   - Above the diagonal (\\(i<j\\)) tile each row \\(i\\) with the horizontal strip \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); this yields another \\(n-1\\) tiles.  \n   - The diagonal squares remain uncovered, giving exactly one uncovered square in each row and column. The total number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n4. **Conclusion:** The lower bound is tight, so the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the set of rows by \\([n]=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the set of columns also by \\([n]\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a function  \n\\[\n\\sigma:[n]\\longrightarrow [n],\\qquad \\sigma(i)=\\text{column of the uncovered square in row }i .\n\\]\nThe condition that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square forces \\(\\sigma\\) to be a bijection; hence \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered by tiles.  \nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; formally a tile is a set\n\\[\nT=[a,b]\\times[c,d]=\\{(i,j)\\mid a\\le i\\le b,\\;c\\le j\\le d\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nTiles are required to be pairwise disjoint, i.e. each unit square belongs to at most one tile.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) write  \n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(L_i\\) is the set of squares left of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\); \\(R_i\\) is the set of squares right of it.  \nThe collection of all squares to be tiled is\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\;\\cup\\;\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 2.1  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) then the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) is covered by a tile whose **rightmost column** equals \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the unique tile \\(T\\) that contains \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\).  \nSince column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) contains the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), the column interval of \\(T\\) cannot contain \\(\\sigma(i)\\); otherwise \\(T\\) would contain an uncovered square, contradicting the definition of a tile.  \nHence the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is at most \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\).  \nBecause \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in T\\), the rightmost column is at least \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\).  \nThus it is exactly \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\). ∎\n\nDefine the set of “left critical columns’’\n\\[\nC_L=\\{\\sigma(i)-1\\mid i\\in[n],\\;\\sigma(i)>1\\}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, exactly one index satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); consequently \\(|C_L|=n-1\\).\n\nBy Lemma 2.1 each column \\(c\\in C_L\\) is the rightmost column of a tile.  \nA tile has a unique rightmost column, so distinct columns of \\(C_L\\) correspond to distinct tiles.  \nHence at least \\(|C_L|=n-1\\) tiles are required.  \nWe call these tiles **left‑type** tiles.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 2.2  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) then the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) is covered by a tile whose **leftmost column** equals \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric to Lemma 2.1. The tile containing \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) cannot extend to column \\(\\sigma(i)\\); therefore its leftmost column is exactly \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\). ∎\n\nDefine the set of “right critical columns’’\n\\[\nC_R=\\{\\sigma(i)+1\\mid i\\in[n],\\;\\sigma(i)<n\\}.\n\\]\nExactly one index satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); thus \\(|C_R|=n-1\\).\n\nBy Lemma 2.2 each column \\(c\\in C_R\\) is the leftmost column of a tile, and distinct columns give distinct tiles.  \nHence at least \\(|C_R|=n-1\\) tiles are required.  \nWe call these tiles **right‑type** tiles.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 2.3  \nNo tile can be simultaneously left‑type and right‑type.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA left‑type tile lies entirely to the left of the uncovered square in each of its rows; formally, if \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) is left‑type then for every \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) we have \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\).  \nA right‑type tile lies entirely to the right of the uncovered square in each of its rows; i.e. for every \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) we have \\(c>\\sigma(i)\\).  \nIf a tile were both left‑type and right‑type, we would have simultaneously \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(c>\\sigma(i)\\) for the same row \\(i\\), which would imply \\(c>d\\), contradicting the definition of a rectangle (\\(c\\le d\\)). ∎\n\nConsequently the sets of left‑type tiles and right‑type tiles are disjoint.  \nTherefore any tiling uses at least\n\\[\n|C_L|+|C_R|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Tiles covering the squares below the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(T_j^{\\text{down}}\\) is a vertical rectangle of width \\(1\\) and height \\(n-j\\); it contains no diagonal square because its rows satisfy \\(i>j\\).  \nThe \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_j^{\\text{down}}\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares with column index smaller than the row index.\n\n*Tiles covering the squares above the diagonal.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(T_i^{\\text{up}}\\) is a horizontal rectangle of height \\(1\\) and width \\(n-i\\); it contains no diagonal square because its columns satisfy \\(j>i\\).  \nThe \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_i^{\\text{up}}\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares with column index larger than the row index.\n\nThe union of the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_j^{\\text{down}}\\}\\cup\\{T_i^{\\text{up}}\\}\\) covers every unit square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  \nEach row \\(i\\) and each column \\(i\\) contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound proved in Section 2 shows that any admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nThe construction of Section 3 achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
