"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. Show that the set of uncovered unit squares must be a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n2. For each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) consider the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\). The tile covering it must have rightmost column \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\); distinct rows give \\(n-1\\) distinct tiles.  \n3. For each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) consider the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\). The tile covering it must have leftmost column \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\); distinct rows give another \\(n-1\\) distinct tiles.  \n4. Prove that a tile cannot be counted in both groups (it cannot be simultaneously left\u2011type and right\u2011type). Hence any admissible tiling uses at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.  \n5. Construct a tiling attaining this bound by taking the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\). Place a vertical \\(1\\times (n-j)\\) tile in each column \\(j\\) below the diagonal and a horizontal \\((n-i)\\times1\\) tile in each row \\(i\\) above the diagonal. This yields exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles and leaves precisely the diagonal squares uncovered.  \n6. Conclude that the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the rows and columns of the grid by the index set \\([n]=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nSince each row contains exactly one unit square that is not covered, we may define a function  \n\\[\n\\sigma:[n]\\longrightarrow [n],\\qquad \\sigma(i)=\\text{column of the uncovered square in row }i .\n\\]\nThe hypothesis that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square implies that \\(\\sigma\\) is injective; because the domain and codomain have the same cardinality, \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, i.e. a permutation of \\([n]\\).  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered by tiles.\n\nA tile is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; we write a tile as\n\\[\nT=[a,b]\\times[c,d]=\\{(i,j)\\mid a\\le i\\le b,\\;c\\le j\\le d\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nTiles are pairwise disjoint: each unit square belongs to at most one tile.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) set\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(L_i\\) consists of the squares left of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\), and \\(R_i\\) those to its right.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 2.1  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) then the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in L_i\\) is covered by a tile whose **rightmost column** equals \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(T\\) be the unique tile containing \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\).  \nBecause column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) contains the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), \\(T\\) cannot contain column \\(\\sigma(i)\\); otherwise it would also contain \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\). Hence the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is at most \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\). Since \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in T\\), the rightmost column is at least \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\). Therefore it is exactly \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\). \u220e\n\nDefine the set of **left\u2011critical columns**\n\\[\nC_L=\\{\\sigma(i)-1\\mid i\\in[n],\\;\\sigma(i)>1\\}.\n\\]\nBecause exactly one index satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\), we have \\(|C_L|=n-1\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2.1 each column \\(c\\in C_L\\) is the rightmost column of a (unique) tile.  \nSince a tile has a unique rightmost column, distinct columns of \\(C_L\\) correspond to distinct tiles.  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required; we call them **left\u2011type** tiles.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 2.2  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) then the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\in R_i\\) is covered by a tile whose **leftmost column** equals \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric to Lemma\u202f2.1. The tile containing \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) cannot contain column \\(\\sigma(i)\\); hence its leftmost column is at least \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\). Since the square lies in the tile, the leftmost column is at most \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\). Thus it equals \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\). \u220e\n\nDefine the set of **right\u2011critical columns**\n\\[\nC_R=\\{\\sigma(i)+1\\mid i\\in[n],\\;\\sigma(i)<n\\}.\n\\]\nExactly one index satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\), so \\(|C_R|=n-1\\).  \nEach column \\(c\\in C_R\\) is the leftmost column of a (unique) tile, and distinct columns give distinct tiles.  \nHence at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles are required; we call them **right\u2011type** tiles.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 2.3  \nNo tile can be both left\u2011type and right\u2011type.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a tile \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) is counted in both Lemma\u202f2.1 and Lemma\u202f2.2.  \nThus there exist rows \\(i,j\\) such that\n\\[\nd=\\sigma(i)-1,\\qquad c=\\sigma(j)+1 .\n\\]\nHence \\(\\sigma(j)+1\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\), i.e. \\(\\sigma(i)\\ge\\sigma(j)+2\\).\n\nBecause \\(T\\) contains \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\), any row \\(k\\) belonging to \\(T\\) must satisfy \\(\\sigma(k)>\\sigma(i)-1\\); otherwise column \\(\\sigma(k)\\) would lie in \\([c,d]\\) and the uncovered square \\((k,\\sigma(k))\\) would be inside \\(T\\), contradicting the definition of a tile. In particular, for row \\(j\\) we would have \\(\\sigma(j)>\\sigma(i)-1\\), which contradicts \\(\\sigma(j)\\le\\sigma(i)-2\\). Hence such a tile cannot exist. \u220e\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2.3 the sets of left\u2011type and right\u2011type tiles are disjoint.  \nTherefore any admissible tiling uses at least\n\\[\n|C_L|+|C_R|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Vertical tiles (below the diagonal).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(V_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\); its sides lie on grid lines.\n\n*Horizontal tiles (above the diagonal).*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(H_i\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\).\n\nThe \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct columns.  \nThe \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows.  \nA vertical tile \\(V_j\\) lies entirely in rows \\(>j\\), while a horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) lies entirely in columns \\(>i\\); thus a vertical tile and a horizontal tile never intersect (otherwise we would need a pair \\((i,j)\\) with simultaneously \\(i>j\\) and \\(i<j\\), impossible).  \n\nConsequently the \\(2n-2\\) tiles \\(\\{V_j\\}\\cup\\{H_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs to the horizontal tile \\(H_i\\); every square with \\(i>j\\) belongs to the vertical tile \\(V_j\\).  \nOnly the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered, providing exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound proved in Section\u202f2 shows that any admissible tiling must contain at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nSection\u202f3 exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
