ously, impossible.\n\nThus they cover all squares except diagonal squares (i,i). So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2 = 4048. So construction works.\n\nThus answer is correct, reasoning uses valid methods: combinatorial argument, counting, injection, bijection, case analysis, construction. No invalid methods.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. Look for any justification gaps or critical errors.\n\nPotential issues: The proof of Lemma 2.3 uses a somewhat informal argument about rows belonging to T. But it's logically sound. Might be a small gap: they claim \"any row k belonging to T must satisfy sigma(k) > sigma(i)-1; otherwise column sigma(k) would lie in [c,d] and the uncovered square (k, sigma(k)) would be inside T\". This is correct: if sigma(k) ≤ sigma(i)-1, then sigma(k) ≤ d (since d = sigma(i)-1) and also sigma(k) ≥ c? Actually c = sigma(j)+1, and sigma(k) could be less than c. But they need to argue that if sigma(k) ≤ d, then column sigma(k) is within the tile's column range [c,d] (since c ≤ sigma(k) ≤ d). However, they didn't guarantee sigma(k) ≥ c. But if sigma(k) ≤ d, but sigma(k) might be less than c. However, if sigma(k) < c, then column sigma(k) is left of the tile's leftmost column, so not inside tile. So the statement \"any row k belonging to T must satisfy sigma(k) > sigma(i)-1\" might be too strong. Actually they need to show that for any row k in T, sigma(k) cannot be ≤ d because that would place uncovered square in tile. But if sigma(k) < c, then uncovered square column is left of tile, not inside tile. So they need to argue that sigma(k) cannot be ≤ d but also cannot be < c? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nTile T = [a,b]×[c,d] (rows a through b, columns c through d). It contains squares (i, sigma(i)-1) and (j, sigma(j)+1). So d = sigma(i)-1, c = sigma(j)+1.\n\nNow consider any row k ∈ [a,b] (i.e., row belonging to tile). The tile includes columns c through d. The uncovered square in row k is at column sigma(k). If sigma(k) is between c and d inclusive, then the uncovered square (k, sigma(k)) would be inside the tile, which is forbidden. So sigma(k) cannot be in [c,d]. So sigma(k) must be either < c or > d. Since d = sigma(i)-1, sigma(k) > d means sigma(k) ≥ sigma(i). Since sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1 (since sigma(i) ≥ sigma(j)+2), we have sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1 = c. So sigma(k) > d implies sigma(k) ≥ sigma(i) > c. So sigma(k) > d is okay. If sigma(k) < c, then sigma(k) ≤ sigma(j). Since sigma(j) = c-1, sigma(k) ≤ c-1 = sigma(j). So sigma(k) ≤ sigma(j). That is possible. So the condition \"sigma(k) > sigma(i)-1\" is not necessary for all rows k; some rows could have sigma(k) < c. So the argument in Lemma 2.3 might be flawed. However, they only need to derive a contradiction for row j. Let's examine the argument: They assume tile T is both left-type and right-type. So there exist rows i and j such that d = sigma(i)-1 and c = sigma(j)+1. Then sigma(j)+1 ≤ sigma(i)-1 => sigma(i) ≥ sigma(j)+2.\n\nNow they claim: Since T contains (i, sigma(i)-1), any row k belonging to T must satisfy sigma(k) > sigma(i)-1; otherwise column sigma(k) would lie in [c,d] and the uncovered square (k, sigma(k)) would be inside T, contradicting the definition of a tile. In particular, for row j we would have sigma(j) > sigma(i)-1, which contradicts sigma(j) ≤ sigma(i)-2.\n\nBut the claim \"any row k belonging to T must satisfy sigma(k) > sigma(i)-1\" is not necessarily true: For row j, sigma(j) < sigma(i)-1 (since sigma(i) ≥ sigma(j)+2). So sigma(j) ≤ sigma(i)-2. So sigma(j) is less than sigma(i)-1. The argument says that if sigma(j) ≤ sigma(i)-1, then column sigma(j) would lie in [c,d]? Let's check: c = sigma(j)+1, d = sigma(i)-1. So [c,d] = [sigma(j)+1, sigma(i)-1]. Since sigma(j) < sigma(j)+1 = c, sigma(j) is not in [c,d]. So uncovered square (j, sigma(j)) is not inside tile T. So the condition that sigma(k) must be > sigma(i)-1 is not necessary; sigma(k) could be less than c. So the proof that tile cannot be both left-type and right-typ