occupy the set of rows \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Its indicator matrix is\n\\[\nB_{IJ}=u\\,v^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\nwhere \\(u\\) (resp. \\(v\\)) is the characteristic column vector of \\(I\\) (resp. \\(J\\)).  Since both \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) are non\u2011zero, \\(\\operatorname{rank}(B_{IJ})=1\\). \u220e\n\n##### Lemma 3.  Any tiling that satisfies the problem\u2019s conditions uses at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  The tiles partition the set of covered squares, i.e.\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{t=1}^{k} B_t,\n\\]\nwhere each \\(B_t\\) is the rank\u20111 matrix associated with the \\(t\\)-th tile and \\(k\\) is the number of tiles.  By subadditivity of rank,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\operatorname{rank}(B_t)=k.\n\\]\nLemma\u202f1 gives \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\), so \\(k\\ge n\\). \u220e\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f3 with \\(n=2025\\) yields the lower bound\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge2025.\n\\]\n\n#### 4. Completion of the proof  \n\nThe construction of Section\u202f2 shows that \\(2025\\) tiles are sufficient, while Lemma\u202f3 shows that fewer than \\(2025\\) tiles are impossible.  Therefore the exact minimum equals\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
