 in column\u202f1) and exactly one equals \\(n-1\\) (the row whose\nuncovered square is in column\u202f\\(n\\)).  \n\nFor every row with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) consider its **rightmost cell** of \\(L\\),\n\n\\[\nc_i=(i,\\ell_i).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) cells are distinct and lie in distinct columns, because the\ncolumn index of \\(c_i\\) equals \\(\\ell_i\\) and the values \\(\\ell_i\\) are all\ndifferent among rows with \\(\\ell_i>0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \n*No rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain two distinct cells \\(c_i\\) and\n\\(c_{i'}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\subseteq L\\) contains \\(c_i=(i,\\ell_i)\\) and\n\\(c_{i'}=(i',\\ell_{i'})\\) with \\(\\ell_i<\\ell_{i'}\\).  Then \\(i,i'\\in I\\) and\n\\(\\ell_i,\\ell_{i'}\\in J\\).  Since row \\(i\\) contains only the columns\n\\(1,\\dots ,\\ell_i\\), the cell \\((i,\\ell_{i'})\\) does **not** belong to \\(L\\).\nNevertheless \\((i,\\ell_{i'})\\) lies in \\(I\\times J\\), contradicting the\nassumption \\(R\\subseteq L\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nBecause the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\{c_i\\}\\) must all be covered and each rectangle of\n\\(L\\) can cover at most one of them, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required\nto cover \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the right region  \n\nThe right region \\(R\\) is symmetric.  For each row with \\(p(i)<n\\) define its\n**leftmost cell** of \\(R\\),\n\n\\[\nd_i=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAgain there are \\(n-1\\) such cells, one in each column.  The same argument as\nin Lemma\u202f2 shows that a rectangle contained in \\(R\\) can contain at most one\n\\(d_i\\).  Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Total lower bound  \n\nAny tiling of the whole board must cover both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\); therefore the\nnumber of tiles satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor the permutation \\(p\\) from (1) place, for each row \\(i\\),\n\n* a horizontal tile covering the cells \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,p(i)-1)\\) if\n  \\(p(i)>1\\);\n* a horizontal tile covering the cells \\((i,p(i)+1),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) if\n  \\(p(i)<n\\).\n\nThese tiles are disjoint because they lie in distinct rows, and none of them\ncontains the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\).  Exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\) (no\nleft\u2011hand tile) and exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\) (no right\u2011hand tile), so the\ntotal number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the lower bound (9) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimal number of tiles  \n\nFrom (9) and the construction (10) we conclude that the minimum possible number\nof tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  For the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
