l unit squares **below** the holes.  \nA tiling of the whole board (except the holes) is precisely a partition of \\(U\\cup D\\) into axis‑parallel rectangles whose sides lie on grid lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Upper region)\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) set  \n\n\\[\nh_j=q(j)-1\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\(\\{h_j\\}_{j=1}^{n}\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nConsider any rectangle \\(R\\subseteq U\\).  \nBecause a rectangle is a product of a set of consecutive rows \\([1,H]\\) and a set of consecutive columns \\([a,b]\\), we must have  \n\n\\[\nH\\le \\min_{j\\in[a,b]}h_j .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the only columns of \\([a,b]\\) whose **topmost** cell \\((h_j,j)\\) lies inside \\(R\\) are those with \\(h_j=H\\).  \nSince the heights \\(h_j\\) are all distinct, there is **at most one** such column. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 1\n\nExactly \\(n-1\\) columns have \\(h_j\\ge1\\); each of them contributes a distinct topmost cell \\((h_j,j)\\) belonging to \\(U\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 each rectangle of a tiling of \\(U\\) can contain at most one of these cells, therefore any tiling of \\(U\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Lower region)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nh'_j=n-q(j)\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(\\{h'_j\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nExactly the same argument as in Lemma 1 shows that any rectangle contained in \\(D\\) can contain at most one topmost cell of a column of \\(D\\).  \nSince there are \\(n-1\\) columns with \\(h'_j\\ge1\\), any tiling of \\(D\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the whole board\n\nEvery tiling of the board (except the uncovered squares) is the union