 Therefore any covering uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n   tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells).  \n\n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the left\u2011type tile  \n     \\[\n     L_k=[k+1,n]\\times[1,k],\n     \\]\n     i.e. rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n     These \\(n-1\\) tiles cover exactly the squares strictly **below** the diagonal.\n\n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the right\u2011type tile  \n     \\[\n     R_k=[1,n-k]\\times[n-k+1,n],\n     \\]\n     i.e. rows \\(1,\\dots ,n-k\\) and columns \\(n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n     These \\(n-1\\) tiles cover exactly the squares strictly **above** the diagonal.\n\n   The two families are disjoint, together they cover every off\u2011diagonal unit square exactly once, and the diagonal squares remain uncovered, giving one uncovered square in each row and each column.\n\n   The total number of tiles used is \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide; thus the minimum possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nNumber the rows and columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of all covered squares is \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}(L_i\\cup R_i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet a rectangular tile occupy rows \\(a,\\dots ,b\\) and columns \\(c,\\dots ,d\\) (\\(a\\le b,\\;c\\le d\\)).  \nThen either \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\) for every \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) (the tile lies completely to the left of the uncovered square in each of its rows) or \\(c>\\sigma(i)\\) for every \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) (the tile lies completely to the right of the uncovered square in each of its rows).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf for some row \\(i\\) we had \\(\\sigma(i)\\in[c,d]\\), the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) would belong to the tile, contradicting the hypothesis that tiles may not cover uncovered squares.  \nHence for each \\(i\\) either \\(\\sigma(i)<c\\) or \\(\\sigma(i)>d\\).  \nIf there were rows \\(i,j\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<c\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)>d\\), then \\(\\sigma(i)<c\\le d<\\sigma(j)\\); consequently the interval \\([c,d]\\) would contain a column between \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)\\).  \nSince the rows \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) both belong to the same contiguous block \\([a,b]\\), some row \\(k\\) between them would have \\(\\sigma(k)\\in[c,d]\\), which is impossible.  \nThus the same alternative holds for all rows of the tile. \u220e\n\nConsequently every tile is of one of the two forms  \n\n\\[\n\\text{left\u2011type: }[a,b]\\times[1,c]\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\n\\text{right\u2011type: }[a,b]\\times[d,n],\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c<\\sigma(i)\\) (resp. \\(d>\\sigma(i)\\)) for all rows \\(i\\) that the tile occupies.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (lower bound for left side)  \nAt least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011type tiles are necessary.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(i_k\\) be the unique index satisfying \\(\\sigma(i_k)=k+1\\) (such an index exists because \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection).  \nThe cell \\((i_k,k)\\) belongs to \\(L_{i_k}\\).  \n\nConsider any left\u2011type tile that covers \\((i_k,k)\\).  \nIts column interval is \\([1,c]\\) for some \\(c\\).  \nIf \\(c<k\\) then the cell \\((i_k,k)\\) would not be covered; if \\(c>k\\) then the column \\(k+1\\) would also belong to the tile, and since \\(\\sigma(i_k)=k+1\\) the uncovered square \\((i_k,k+1)\\) would be covered \u2013 a contradiction.  \nHence the rightmost column of the tile must be exactly \\(c=k\\).\n\nTwo different values of \\(k\\) give two different rightmost columns, therefore they correspond to two different tiles.  \nThus we need at least one distinct left\u2011type tile for each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\); i.e. at least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011type tiles. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (lower bound for right side)  \nAt least \\(n-1\\) right\u2011type tiles are necessary.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let \\(j_k\\) be the unique index with \\(\\sigma(j_k)=k-1\\).  \nThe cell \\((j_k,k)\\) lies in \\(R_{j_k}\\).  \n\nA right\u2011type tile covering this cell has column interval \\([d,n]\\).  \nIf \\(d>k\\) the cell \\((j_k,k)\\) would be uncovered; if \\(d<k\\) then column \\(k-1\\) would also be inside the tile, contradicting the fact that \\((j_k,k-1)\\) is uncovered"
