(i,k)\\) with \\(k<i\\le n\\) (the \u201cbelow\u2019\u2019 block).  \n\n   When \\(k=1\\) the first rectangle is empty, and when \\(k=n\\) the second rectangle is empty; in those two columns only one rectangle is placed.  \n   The rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each of them avoids the diagonal cell \\((k,k)\\), and together they cover every off\u2011diagonal cell exactly once.  \n   The total number of rectangles used is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound shows that no placement can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nNumber the rows and columns of the grid by \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares\n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square and each column also contains exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell  \n\n\\[\n(i,\\sigma(i)) .\n\\]\n\nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na permutation matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nFix a column \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n)\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nr=\\sigma^{-1}(k)\n\\]\n\nbe the row of the uncovered square in this column; i.e. \\((r,k)\\in U\\).\n\nAll other cells of column \\(k\\) are the \\(n-1\\) squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,k)\\mid i\\neq r\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsider any rectangular tile that contains a cell of column \\(k\\).  \nSince the tile\u2019s column interval must contain \\(k\\), the tile\u2019s row interval must **avoid** the row \\(r\\); otherwise the tile would also contain the uncovered square \\((r,k)\\), which is forbidden.  \nConsequently every such tile lies completely\n\n* either in rows \\(1,2,\\dots ,r-1\\) (above the uncovered square), or\n* in rows \\(r+1,r+2,\\dots ,n\\) (below the uncovered square).\n\nHence the cells of column \\(k\\) split into two (possibly empty) contiguous blocks:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{above }&: \\{(i,k)\\mid 1\\le i< r\\},\\\\\n\\text{below }&: \\{(i,k)\\mid r< i\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach non\u2011empty block must be covered by at least one tile, because a tile cannot cross the row \\(r\\).\n\nIf \\(r=1\\) (the uncovered square is in the top row) the \u201cabove\u2019\u2019 block is empty, so column \\(k\\) needs **one** tile.  \nIf \\(r=n\\) (the uncovered square is in the bottom row) the \u201cbelow\u2019\u2019 block is empty, again only one tile is needed.  \n\nFor every other column (\\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)) both blocks are non\u2011empty, so at least **two** tiles are required.\n\nSince exactly one column contains the uncovered square of the top row (\\(k=\\sigma(1)\\)) and exactly one column contains the uncovered square of the bottom row (\\(k=\\sigma(n)\\)), we obtain the following lower bound for the total number \\(T\\) of tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &\\ge 2\\,(n-2) \\;+\\; 1 \\;+\\; 1 \\\\\n  &= 2n-2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A placement attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each column \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n\\)) place the following vertical tiles:\n\n* **Above\u2011tile** \\(A_k\\): the rectangle consisting of all cells \\((i,k)\\) with \\(1\\le i<k\\).  \n  (If \\(k=1\\) this tile is empty and is omitted.)\n\n* **Below\u2011tile** \\(B_k\\): the rectangle consisting of all cells \\((i,k)\\) with \\(k<i\\le n\\).  \n  (If \\(k=n\\) this tile is empty and is omitted.)\n\nEach tile is a genuine rectangle: its rows form a contiguous interval and its columns consist of the single column \\(k\\).  \nBecause the only uncovered cell in column \\(k\\) is \\((k,k)\\), neither \\(A_k\\) nor \\(B_k\\) contains an uncovered square.  \n\nThe family \\(\\{A_k,B_k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n\\}\\) (with the two empty tiles omitted) covers every off\u2011diagonal cell exactly once:\n\n* a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) lies in column \\(j\\);\n* if \\(i<j\\) it belongs to the above\u2011tile \\(A_j\\);\n* if \\(i>j\\) it belongs to the below\u2011tile \\(B_j\\).\n\nThe tiles are pairwise disjoint, and the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered, providing exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.\n\nThe number of tiles used is\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{above\u2011tiles }A_2,\\dots ,A_n}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{below\u2011tiles }B_1,\\dots ,B_{n-1}}\n\\;=\\;2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality\n\nEquation \\((1)\\) shows that no arrangement can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while equation \\((2)\\) exhibits an arrangement that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
