\n   i.e. column \\(c\\) together with all rows strictly **below** the hole.\n   These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every\n   square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<\\pi(i)\\) (the whole “left’’ part of the board).\n\n   – For each row \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   H_r=\\{(r,c):\\pi(r)<c\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. row \\(r\\) together with all columns strictly **right** of its hole.\n   These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every\n   square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>\\pi(i)\\) (the whole “right’’ part).\n\n   The two families are disjoint because a column used in a vertical rectangle\n   is always \\(\\le n-1\\) while a row used in a horizontal rectangle is always\n   \\(\\le n-1\\); consequently no cell belongs to both a vertical and a horizontal\n   rectangle.  Altogether we obtain a tiling with exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\)\n   rectangles.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the minimum\n   possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Number the rows and columns of the board by\n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  A placement of the tiles must leave exactly one uncovered\nsquare in each row and each column.  Consequently the set of uncovered squares\nis a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  The squares\n\\((i,\\pi(i))\\) are called **holes**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Critical cells\n\nFor each hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,\\pi(i)-1) & \\text{if }\\pi(i)>1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{undefined} & \\text{if }\\pi(i)=1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nA_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i-1,\\pi(i)) & \\text{if }i>1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{undefined} & \\text{if }i=1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_i\\) is the unit square immediately **to the left** of the hole in row\n\\(i\\) (when such a square exists), and \\(A_i\\) is the unit square immediately\n**above** the hole in column \\(\\pi(i)\\) (when such a square exists).\n\nBecause exactly one hole lies in column 1, there is precisely one index \\(i\\) with\n\\(\\pi(i)=1\\); for this index \\(L_i\\) is undefined.  Hence the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_i:\\pi(i)>1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n-1\\) squares.  Similarly, because exactly one hole lies in\nrow 1, the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A=\\{A_i:i>1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n-1\\) squares.  The two sets are disjoint: every element of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) has column \\(\\pi(i)-1<\\pi(i)\\), whereas every element of\n\\(\\mathcal A\\) has column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C:=\\mathcal L\\cup\\mathcal A\n\\]\n\nhas cardinality  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal C|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll squares of \\(\\mathcal C\\) are covered (they are not holes).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma – a rectangle contains at most one critical cell\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\text{Let }R=I\\times J\\text{ be an axis‑parallel rectangle\nthat contains no hole. Then }|R\\cap\\mathcal C|\\le 1.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(I=\\{r,r+1,\\dots ,r+\\ell-1\\}\\) and \\(J=\\{c,c+1,\\dots ,c+m-1\\}\\) with\n\\(\\ell,m\\ge1\\).\n\n*Case 1 – two left‑neighbour cells.*  \nSuppose \\(R\\) contained \\(L_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\(L_{i'}=(i',\\pi(i')-1)\\) with\n\\(i\\neq i'\\).  Then \\(\\pi(i)\\neq\\pi(i')\\) and the columns\n\\(\\pi(i)-1\\) and \\(\\pi(i')-1\\) are distinct.  Because \\(J\\) is an interval,\nit contains both columns, hence also the column \\(\\max\\{\\pi(i),\\pi(i')\\}\\).\nSince the corresponding row (either \\(i\\) or \\(i'\\)) belongs to \\(I\\), the\nhole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) or \\((i',\\pi(i'))\\) lies inside \\(R\\), contradicting the\nassumption that \\(R\\) avoids all holes.\n\n*Case 2 – two above‑neighbour cells.*  \nIf \\(R\\) contained \\(A_i=(i-1,\\pi(i))\\) and \\(A_{i'}=(i'-1,\\pi(i'))\\) with\n\\(i\\neq i'\\), then the rows \\(i-1\\) and \\(i'-1\\) are distinct.  Since \\(I\\) is an\ninterval, it contains both rows and therefore also the row\n\\(\\max\\{i,i'\\}\\).  The column of the corresponding hole belongs to \\(J\\);\nhence that hole would be inside \\(R\\), a contradiction.\n\n*Case 3 – one left‑neighbour and one above‑neighbour.*  \nAssume \\(R\\) contains \\(L_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\(A_{i'}=(i'-1,\\pi(i'))\\).\nThen \\(i\\in I,\\; \\pi(i)-1\\in J\\) and \\(i'-1\\in I,\\; \\pi(i')\\in J\\).\n\nIf \\(\\pi(i')\\le\\pi(i)-1\\), then \\(\\pi(i')<\\pi(i)\\).  Because \\(I\\) is an\ninterval and contains both \\(i'-1\\) and \\(i\\), it also contains the row\n\\(i'\\).  Consequently the hole \\((i',\\pi(i'))\\) lies in \\(R\\), impossible.\n\nHence \\(\\pi(i')>\\pi(i)-1\\).  Since \\(J\\) contains the two columns\n\\(\\pi(i)-1\\) and \\(\\pi(i')\\), it must contain the whole interval\n\\([\\pi(i)-1,\\pi(i')]\\) and therefore the column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  Because\n\\(i\\in I\\), the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) would be inside \\(R\\), again a\ncontradiction.\n\nAll three cases are impossible, so a hole‑free rectangle can meet at most one\nelement of \\(\\mathcal C\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nEvery tile is a rectangle that avoids the holes; by the Lemma it can cover at\nmost one square of \\(\\mathcal C\\).  Since all squares of \\(\\mathcal C\\) must be\ncovered, any tiling needs at least \\(|\\mathcal C|\\) tiles.  Using (1),\n\n\\[\n\\text{number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFix the permutation \\(\\pi\\) that determines the holes.\n\n*Vertical rectangles (covering the “left’’ part).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(r_c=\\pi^{-1}(c)\\) be the row of\nthe hole in that column.  Define  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(r,c): r_c<r\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_c\\) is a vertical rectangle consisting of column \\(c\\) together with all\nrows **strictly below** the hole \\((r_c,c)\\).  The rectangles\n\\(V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy different\ncolumns.  If a square \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(j<\\pi(i)\\) (i.e. it lies to the left\nof its hole), then \\(\\pi(i)>j\\); consequently \\(i>\\pi^{-1}(j)=r_j\\) and\n\\((i,j)\\in V_j\\).  Hence the \\(n-1\\) vertical rectangles cover **all** squares\nlying left of their holes.\n\n*Horizontal rectangles (covering the “right’’ part).*  \nFor each row \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_r=\\{(r,c):\\pi(r)<c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_r\\) is a horizontal rectangle consisting of row \\(r\\) together with all\ncolumns **strictly right** of its hole \\((r,\\pi(r))\\).  The rectangles\n\\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy different\nrows.  If a square \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(j>\\pi(i)\\) (i.e. it lies to the right\nof its hole), then \\(\\pi(i)<j\\) and therefore \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\).  Thus the\n\\(n-1\\) horizontal rectangles cover **all** squares lying right of their holes.\n\nThe two families are disjoint: a vertical rectangle uses only columns\n\\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\) and rows strictly larger than the row of the corresponding\nhole, whereas a horizontal rectangle uses only rows\n\\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\) and columns strictly larger than the column of the\ncorresponding hole.  Consequently no square belongs to both a vertical and a\nhorizontal rectangle.\n\nAltogether we have \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles that cover every square\nexcept the \\(n\\) holes.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\text{number of tiles}\\;\\le\\;2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality\n\nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain equality:\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\), therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
