nConsider two consecutive rows \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\).  \nIf a rectangle \\(T\\) contains a point of type \\(L\\) in row \\(i\\) and a point of type \\(R\\) in row \\(i+1\\), then \\(T\\) cannot also contain any point of row \\(i+1\\) to the left of the hole \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\); otherwise it would contain the hole.  \nConsequently such a rectangle must **stop** at row \\(i\\); it cannot extend to row \\(i+1\\).\n\nThus, moving from row \\(1\\) down to row \\(n\\), each time the type of covered cells changes from \\(L\\) to \\(R\\) (or vice‑versa) we necessarily start a new rectangle.  \nSince the first row is covered by a rectangle of one fixed type, there must be at least one rectangle for that first row, and at least one new rectangle for each of the \\(n-1\\) transitions between consecutive rows.  \nHence any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n1+(n-1)=n+1\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(n+1\\) rectangles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (any permutation that is monotone increasing works equally well).  \nThe holes are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left‑hand rectangles.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) is a (possibly empty) rectangle lying completely to the left of the hole in row \\(i\\).  \nThe \\(n\\) rectangles \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover every cell that lies strictly left of the main diagonal.\n\n*Right‑hand rectangle.*  \nAll cells that lie strictly to the right of the diagonal form the set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the holes are exactly the diagonal cells, none of them belongs to \\(R\\).  \nConsequently \\(R\\) itself is a rectangle (its left side is the line \\(j=i+1\\) and its right side is the line \\(j=n\\)); it covers every cell strictly right of the diagonal and does not interse