a(r+1)\\}\\) lacks the column \\(\\sigma(r+1)\\), which is present in row \\(r\\) (since \\(\\sigma(r+1)>r\\)).  \nHence the rectangle cannot contain the cell \\((r,\\sigma(r+1))\\), and consequently it cannot be extended from row \\(r\\) to row \\(r+1\\).  \n\nThus a new rectangle must start at each of the \\(n-1\\) transitions \\(r=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover all cells of the top part \\(\\bigcup_j T_j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the bottom part \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n}B_j\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nD_r=\\{\\,j\\mid r>r_j\\,\\},\n\\qquad 1\\le r\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_r\\) is the set of columns whose bottom segment contains row \\(r\\).  \nExactly the same reasoning as above gives  \n\n\\[\n|D_r|=r,\\qquad \nD_{r}=D_{r-1}\\cup\\{\\sigma(r)\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nso when we move upward from row \\(r\\) to row \\(r-1\\) exactly one new column (the column \\(\\sigma(r)\\)) becomes available.  \nRepeating the argument of Section\u202f1 with the sets \\(D_r\\) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover all cells of the bottom part \\(\\bigcup_j B_j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nThe top part and the bottom part are disjoint, therefore any tiling of the whole board must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe holes are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*For the lower triangle.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{low}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R^{\\text{low}}_j\\) consists of all cells of column \\(j\\) that lie **below** the diagonal; it does not contain any hole because the only hole in column \\(j\\) is at row \\(j\\).\n\n*For the upper triangle.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{up}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R^{\\text{up}}_j\\) consists of all cells of column \\(j\\) that lie **above** the diagonal; it avoids the hole \\((j,j)\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{R^{\\text{low}}_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R^{\\text{up}}_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal cell of the board exactly once.  \nThe total number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion for the given board\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\) we obtain the minimal possible number of tiles\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
