igma^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nbe the row of the hole in column \\(j\\).  \nThe cells of column \\(j\\) are split by the hole into two vertical segments\n\n\\[\nT_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<r_j\\}\\qquad\\text{(the “top’’ segment)},\n\\]\n\\[\nB_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j<i\\le n\\}\\qquad\\text{(the “bottom’’ segment)} .\n\\]\n\nA rectangle cannot contain a hole; therefore any rectangle that contains a cell of column \\(j\\) must be completely contained either in \\(T_j\\) or in \\(B_j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the top part \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n}T_j\\)\n\nFor a fixed row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_r=\\{\\,j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid r<r_j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(C_r\\) is precisely the set of columns whose top segment contains the cell \\((r,j)\\).  \nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, each integer \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) occurs exactly once among the values \\(r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n|C_r|=n-r,\\qquad \nC_{r+1}=C_r\\setminus\\{\\sigma(r+1)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence when we pass from row \\(r\\) to the next row \\(r+1\\) exactly one column (namely \\(\\sigma(r+1)\\)) disappears from the set of columns that are still available.\n\nConsider any rectangle \\(R\\) that lies completely inside the top part \\(\\bigcup_j T_j\\).  \nLet the rows of \\(R\\) be the interval \\([a,b]\\) (\\(a\\le b\\)).  \nBecause \\(R\\) must avoid every hole, for each column \\(j\\) used by \\(R\\) we must have \\([a,b]\\subseteq T_j\\); equivalently \\(b<r_j\\).  \nThus the set of columns that can appear together with the whole row interval \\([a,b]\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcap_{r=a}^{b}C_r = C_b,\n\\]\n\nusing the nesting property (1).  \nTherefore a rectangle that contains rows \\(a,\\dots ,b\\) can use only columns from \\(C_b\\).\n\nNow look at the transition from row \\(r\\) to row \\(r+1\\).  \nIf a rectangle covered both rows \\(r\\) and \\(r+1\\), its column set would have to be contained in \\(C_{r+1}\\).  \nBut \\(C_{r+1}=C_r\\setminus\\{\\sigma(r+1)\\}\\) lacks the column \\(\\sigma(r+1)\\), which is present in row \\(r\\) (since \\(\\sigma(r+1)>r\\)).  \nHence the rectangle cannot contain the cell \\((r,\\sigma(r+1))\\), and consequently it cannot be extended from row \\(r\\) to row \\(r+1\\).  \n\nThus a new rectangle must start at each of the \\(n-1\\) transitions \\(r=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover all cells of the top part \\(\\bigcup_j T_j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the bottom part \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n}B_j\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nD_r=\\{\\,j\\mid r>r_j\\,\\},\n\\qquad 1\\le r\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_r\\) is the set of columns whose bottom segment contains row \\(r\\).  \nExactly the same reasoning as above gives  \n\n\\[\n|D_r|=r,\\qquad \nD_{r}=D_{r-1}\\cup\\{\\sigma(r)\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nso when we move upward from row \\(r\\) to row \\(r-1\\) exactly one new column (the column \\(\\sigma(r)\\)) becomes available.  \nRepeating the argument of Section 1 with the sets \\(D_r\\) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover all cells of the bottom part \\(\\bigcup_j B_j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nThe top part and the bottom part are disjoint, therefore any tiling of the whole board must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe holes are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*For the lower triangle.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{low}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R^{\\text{low}}_j\\) consists of all cells of column \\(j\\) that lie **below** the diagonal; it does not contain any hole because the only hole in column \\(j\\) is at row \\(j\\).\n\n*For the upper triangle.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{up}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R^{\\text{up}}_j\\) consists of all cells of column \\(j\\) that lie **above** the diagonal; it avoids the hole \\((j,j)\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{R^{\\text{low}}_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R^{\\text{up}}_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every non‑diagonal cell of the board exactly once.  \nThe total number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion for the given board\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\) we obtain the minimal possible number of tiles\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Ga