\nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.\n\nDefine for each row  \n\n\\[\nL_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad(0\\le L_i\\le n-1),\\qquad \nR_i:=n-p(i)=n-1-L_i .\n\\]\n\nThus row \\(i\\) consists of a left block of \\(L_i\\) covered squares, then the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\), then a right block of \\(R_i\\) covered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Tiles lie entirely in one side region\n\nConsider any rectangular tile \\(T\\).  \nLet its rows be the interval \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and its columns the interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) (both inclusive).  \nIf for some row \\(i\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) we had \\(c_1\\le p(i)\\le c_2\\), then the tile would cover the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\), contradicting the problem statement.  \nHence for every row \\(i\\) intersected by the tile we must have either  \n\n\\[\nc_2\\le p(i)-1=L_i\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad c_1\\ge p(i)+1=L_i+2 .\n\\]\n\nSince the column interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) is the same for all rows of the tile, it cannot satisfy the first inequality for some rows and the second for others.  \nConsequently the whole tile is contained either in  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le L_i\\}\n\\]\n\n(the **left side region**) or in  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;L_i+2\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\n(the **right side region**). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound for the left side region\n\nObserve that the multiset \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) because the values \\(p(i)=L_i+1\\) run through all numbers \\(1,\\dots ,n\\).  \nHence exactly one row has \\(L_i=0\\) (the hole at column 1) and the remaining \\(n-1\\) rows have \\(L_i>0\\).  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S:=\\{(i,L_i):L_i>0\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is \\(|\\mathcal S|=n-1\\) and the points of \\(\\mathcal S\\) have **distinct column coordinates** (the numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\)).  \n\nLet \\(R\\subseteq\\mathcal L\\) be any rectangle.  \nSuppose \\(R\\) contains two distinct points \\((i_1,L_{i_1})\\) and \\((i_2,L_{i_2})\\) of \\(\\mathcal S\\) with, say, \\(L_{i_1}>L_{i_2}\\).  \nSince both points belong to \\(R\\), the rightmost column of \\(R\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{right}(R)\\ge L_{i_1}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(R\\subseteq\\mathcal L\\) forces \\(\\operatorname{right}(R)\\le L_{i_2}\\) for row \\(i_2\\), a contradiction.  \nThus **no rectangle in \\(\\mathcal L\\) can contain two points of \\(\\mathcal S\\)**.  \n\nSince each rectangle can cover at most one point of \\(\\mathcal S\\) and \\(|\\mathcal S|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to tile \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the right side region\n\nAnalogously, the rows with a non‑empty right block are those with \\(L_i<n-1\\); there are again \\(n-1\\) such rows.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T:=\\{(i,L_i+2):L_i<n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(\\mathcal T\\) have distinct column coordinates \\(2,3,\\dots ,n\\).  \nThe same argument as in §2 shows that any rectangle contained in \\(\\mathcal R\\) can contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal T\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Universal lower bound\n\nEvery tiling of the whole board consists of a collection of rectangles covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) and a collection covering \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nFrom §§2–3 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the **identity permutation** \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen \\(L_i=i-1\\) and the left side region becomes the strict lower triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<i\\},\n\\]\n\nwhile the right side region becomes the strict upper triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Left triangle.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(i,j):i>k,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-k)\\times k\\).  \nIf \\((i,j)\\in\\mathcal L\\) then \\(j<i\\); setting \\(k=j\\) gives \\(i>k\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le k\\), so \\((i,j)\\in T_j\\).  \nConversely, a point belonging to two distinct \\(T_{k_1}\\) and \\(T_{k_2}\\) would have two different column values, impossible.  \nHence \\(\\{T_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions \\(\\mathcal L\\) into exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Right triangle.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=\\{(i,j):i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\(k\\times (n-k)\\).  \nIf \\((i,j)\\in\\mathcal R\\) then \\(i<j\\); setting \\(k=i\\) yields \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(k+1\\le j\\), so \\((i,j)\\in S_i\\).  \nAgain the sets \\(S_k\\) are pairwise disjoint, giving a partition of \\(\\mathcal R\\) into \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nThus the identity permutation yields a tiling with exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\n\\]\n\nrectangular tiles, and the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), one per row and column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nTherefore the minimal possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2(n-1)}.\n\\]\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2(2025-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048,\n\\]\n\nso Matilda needs at least, and can achieve, **4048** tiles. ∎\n\n---\n\n#