                "content": ".### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{22}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a permutation matrix.**  \n   The condition “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square’’ forces the uncovered squares to be a set  \n   \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (here \\(n=2025\\)).  \n   We may assume \\(\\sigma\\) is the identity, i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound (at least \\(2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) tiles).**  \n   - For each tile we record, for every index \\(i\\), whether \\(i\\) belongs to the *column* side of the tile (value 1) or not (value 0).  \n   - The family of binary vectors \\(\\{v_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\) obtained this way must satisfy: for any distinct \\(i,j\\) there is a coordinate where \\(v_i=0\\) and \\(v_j=1\\) (otherwise the ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) would not be covered).  \n   - Hence the vectors form a separating family for the total order, which forces at least \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) coordinates for the “right‑side’’ of tiles that point from a smaller index to a larger one.  \n   - By symmetry the same number is needed for tiles that point from a larger index to a smaller one.  \n   - Consequently any partition of the off‑diagonal squares into axis‑parallel rectangles needs at least  \n\n     \\[\n     2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\n     \\]\n\n     tiles.\n\n3. **Upper bound (construction with exactly \\(2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) tiles).**  \n   - Use a recursive divide‑and‑conquer on the index set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   - If \\(n=1\\) there is nothing to cover.  \n   - For \\(n>1\\) split \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) into two intervals  \n     \\(L=\\{1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\) and \\(R=\\{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   - Place two tiles: the rectangle \\(L\\times R\\) (rows in \\(L\\), columns in \\(R\\)) and the rectangle \\(R\\times L\\).  \n     Both are contiguous, avoid the diagonal, and cover all squares with a row in one half and a column in the other half.  \n   - Recur on the two sub‑boards \\(L\\times L\\) and \\(R\\times R\\), which contain the diagonal squares of the original board.  \n   - The recursion depth is \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\); at each level we add exactly two tiles.  \n   - Hence the total number of tiles produced is  \n\n     \\[\n     2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n     \\]\n\n   - For \\(n=2025\\) we have \\(\\lceil\\log_2 2025\\rceil=11\\) (since \\(2^{11}=2048>2025\\)), giving \\(2\\cdot11=22\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction match, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2\\lceil\\log_2 2025\\rceil=22\\).  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA unit square is identified with its row and column indices \\((i,j)\\) where \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to a permutation matrix\n\nEach row must contain exactly one uncovered square, and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *perfect matching* between rows and columns; i.e. there exists a permutation\n\\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe choice of \\(\\sigma\\) is free; we may rename the columns so that \\(\\sigma\\) becomes the identity.  \nThus we may assume\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe main diagonal of the board.  \nAll other squares, i.e. the set  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\neq j\\},\n\\]\n\nmust be covered by tiles.  A tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; therefore a tile is a product\n\\(R\\times C\\) where \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) are **intervals** of consecutive rows and consecutive columns, respectively,\nand \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\) (otherwise a diagonal square would be covered).\n\nHence the problem is:\n\n> Partition the set \\(C\\) into axis‑parallel rectangles \\(R_t\\times C_t\\) (\\(t=1,\\dots ,m\\)) such that each\n> \\(R_t\\) and \\(C_t\\) are intervals, \\(R_t\\cap C_t=\\varnothing\\), and the rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n> Determine the smallest possible \\(m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound\n\nFor a rectangle \\(R_t\\times C_t\\) write  \n\n\\[\n\\chi_t(i)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }i\\in C_t,\\\\[2pt]\n0 &\\text{if }i\\in R_t\\text{ or }i\\notin R_t\\cup C_t .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus each index \\(i\\) receives a binary vector  \n\n\\[\nv(i)=(\\chi_1(i),\\chi_2(i),\\dots ,\\chi_m(i))\\in\\{0,1\\}^m .\n\\]\n\nConsider two distinct indices \\(i<j\\).  \nThe square \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(C\\); therefore it must lie in exactly one rectangle, say the \\(t\\)-th one.\nFor that rectangle we have \\(i\\in R_t\\) and \\(j\\in C_t\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\chi_t(i)=0,\\qquad \\chi_t(j)=1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus for every ordered pair \\(i<j\\) there exists a coordinate \\(t\\) where the binary vector of \\(i\\) has a 0\nand the binary vector of \\(j\\) has a 1.  This property is exactly the definition of a *separating family* for the total order\n\\(1<2<\\dots <n\\).\n\nA separating family on \\(n\\) elements needs at least \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) coordinates:\nwith \\(m\\) coordinates there are at most \\(2^{m}\\) distinct binary vectors, while we need \\(n\\) distinct vectors.\nHence\n\n\\[\nm_{\\text{forward}}\\ge \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument applied to the opposite orientation (rectangles with rows to the right of columns) yields another\nset of at least \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) rectangles.  Since a rectangle cannot serve both orientations simultaneously,\nthe total number of rectangles satisfies\n\n\\[\nm\\ge 2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\lceil\\log_2 2025\\rceil = 11\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nm\\ge 2\\cdot 11 = 22 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  An explicit construction with \\(2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) tiles\n\nWe now show that \\(22\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\nDefine a recursive procedure \\(\\text{Cover}(I)\\) where \\(I\\) is an interval of row (and column) indices.\n\n*If \\(|I|=1\\)*, there is no off‑diagonal square inside \\(I\\times I\\); do nothing.\n\n*If \\(|I|>1\\)*, let  \n\n\\[\nL = \\bigl[\\; \\min I,\\; \\min I + \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{|I|}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor -1 \\bigr],\\qquad\nR = \\bigl[\\; \\min I + \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{|I|}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor,\\; \\max I \\bigr].\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are two disjoint consecutive intervals whose union is \\(I\\).\n\nPlace the two tiles  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,L\\times R\\,},\\qquad \\boxed{\\,R\\times L\\,},\n\\]\n\nwhich are contiguous rectangles, avoid the diagonal (because any element of \\(L\\) is strictly smaller than any element of \\(R\\)),\nand together cover **all** squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in L,\\;j\\in R\\) or \\(i\\in R,\\;j\\in L\\).\n\nFinally recurse on the two sub‑boards \\(L\\times L\\) and \\(R\\times R\\) by calling \\(\\text{Cover}(L)\\) and \\(\\text{Cover}(R)\\).\n\nThe recursion depth is \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\): each call halves the size of the interval (up to rounding).  \nAt every level we add exactly two tiles, and the recursion terminates when the interval size becomes \\(1\\).  \nHence the total number of tiles produced is\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot 11 = 22\\) tiles.  \nAll tiles are axis‑parallel, pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every off‑diagonal square.\nThe uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), one per row and one per column, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Optimality\n\nThe lower bound (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(22\\) rectangles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(22\\).  \nTherefore \\(22\\) is the minimal possible number of tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe minimum number of rectangular tiles that Matilda must place so that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{22}.\n\\]"
