utive rows and consecutive columns, respectively,\nand \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\) (otherwise a diagonal square would be covered).\n\nHence the problem is:\n\n> Partition the set \\(C\\) into axis‑parallel rectangles \\(R_t\\times C_t\\) (\\(t=1,\\dots ,m\\)) such that each\n> \\(R_t\\) and \\(C_t\\) are intervals, \\(R_t\\cap C_t=\\varnothing\\), and the rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n> Determine the smallest possible \\(m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound\n\nFor a rectangle \\(R_t\\times C_t\\) write  \n\n\\[\n\\chi_t(i)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }i\\in C_t,\\\\[2pt]\n0 &\\text{if }i\\in R_t\\text{ or }i\\notin R_t\\cup C_t .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus each index \\(i\\) receives a binary vector  \n\n\\[\nv(i)=(\\chi_1(i),\\chi_2(i),\\dots ,\\chi_m(i))\\in\\{0,1\\}^m .\n\\]\n\nConsider two distinct indices \\(i<j\\).  \nThe square \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(C\\); therefore it must lie in exactly one rectangle, say the \\(t\\)-th one.\nFor that rectangle we have \\(i\\in R_t\\) and \\(j\\in C_t\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\chi_t(i)=0,\\qquad \\chi_t(j)=1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus for every ordered pair \\(i<j\\) there exists a coordinate \\(t\\) where the binary vector of \\(i\\) has a 0\nand the binary vector of \\(j\\) has a 1.  This property is exactly the definition of a *separating family* for the total order\n\\(1<2<\\dots <n\\).\n\nA separating family on \\(n\\) elements needs at least \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) coordinates:\nwith \\(m\\) coordinates there are at most \\(2^{m}\\) distinct binary vectors, while we need \\(n\\) distinct vectors.\nHence\n\n\\[\nm_{\\text{forward}}\\ge \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument applied to the opposite orientation (rectangles with rows to the right of columns) yields another\nset of at least \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) rectangles.  Since a rectangle cannot serve both orientations simultaneously,\nthe total number of rectangles satisfies\n\n\\[\nm\\ge 2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\lceil\\log_2 2025\\rceil = 11\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nm\\ge 2\\cdot 11 = 22 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  An explicit construction with \\(2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) tiles\n\nWe now show that \\(22\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\nDefine a recursive procedure \\(\\text{Cover}(I)\\) where \\(I\\) is an interval of row (and column) indices.\n\n*If \\(|I|=1\\)*, there is no off‑diagonal square inside \\(I\\times I\\); do nothing.\n\n*If \\(|I|>1\\)*, let  \n\n\\[\nL = \\bigl[\\; \\min I,\\; \\min I + \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{|I|}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor -1 \\bigr],\\qquad\nR = \\bigl[\\; \\min I + \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{|I|}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor,\\; \\max I \\bigr].\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are two disjoint consecutive intervals whose union is \\(I\\).\n\nPlace the two tiles  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,L\\times R\\,},\\qquad \\boxed{\\,R\\times L\\,},\n\\]\n\nwhich are contiguous rectangles, avoid the diagonal (because any element of \\(L\\) is strictly smaller than any element of \\(R\\)),\nand together cover **all** squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in L,\\;j\\in R\\) or \\(i\\in R,\\;j\\in L\\).\n\nFinally recurse on the two sub‑boards \\(L\\times L\\) and \\(R\\times R\\) by calling \\(\\text{Cover}(L)\\) and \\(\\text{Cover}(R)\\).\n\nThe recursion depth is \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\): each call halves the size of the interval (up to rounding).  \nAt every level we add exactly two tiles, and the recursion terminates when the interval size becomes \\(1\\).  \nHence the total number of tiles produced is\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot 11 = 22\\) tiles.  \nAll tiles are axis‑parallel, pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every off‑diagonal square.\nThe uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), one per row and one per column, as requir